<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tisza</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tisa, Theiss</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tisza is one of the main rivers of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and flowing for about 960 kilometres through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Its broad plain was home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iazyges">Iazyges</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and was settled by the Hungarians in the 9th century. Extensive regulation works in the 19th century shortened and tamed it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tisza.jpg" alt="Tisza" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Váradi Zsolt 10:36, 2 Jun 2005 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Szeged-tisza3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
